<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mu'nis al-Muzaffar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 845–<a href="/years/933">933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Mu'nis al-Khadim, Abu'l-Hasan Mu'nis al-Qushuri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mu'nis al-Muzaffar, also called al-Khadim, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">Eunuch</a>, was commander-in-chief of the Abbasid army from 908 until his death in 933 and virtual dictator and king-maker of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> from 928. A Byzantine Greek eunuch slave, he served the future caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> from the 880s, was banished to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 901, and was recalled in 908 by al-Muqtadir, whom he saved from a palace coup that December. He raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, defeated Fatimid invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 915 and 920 and saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> in 927. After a confrontation in 931–932 that ended with al-Muqtadir's death in battle, he installed al-Qahir, who had him executed in August 933.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the fourteenth-century historian al-Dhahabi, Mu'nis was 90 years old when he died. He was called al-Khadim to tell him apart from his contemporary, the treasurer Mu'nis al-Fahl. Though a harem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a>, he first appears as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> of the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> during the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a> in 880/1, and by 900 he was chief of police of the field army, deputy to the commander-in-chief Badr al-Mu'tadidi. His banishment in 901 was probably tied to the struggle between Badr and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> al-Qasim ibn Ubayd Allah, and possibly to harem intrigues in favour of al-Muqtadir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After he defended the Hasani Palace against the coup of December 908, which aimed to put Abdallah ibn al-Mu'tazz on the throne, al-Muqtadir's mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaghab">Shaghab</a> gave him command of the caliphal guard, the Hujariyya. The vizier Ibn al-Furat, hostile to his rise, kept him on the frontiers. He raided Byzantine Asia Minor from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Malatya</a> in 909, recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> and supervised prisoner exchanges with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. His victory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 915 earned him the title al-Muzaffar, the Victorious. In 918 he was defeated before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> by the Sajid Yusuf ibn Abi'l-Saj, but beat him there the next year and took him captive to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><p>He was a staunch rival of Ibn al-Furat and ally of Ali ibn Isa al-Jarrah. During Ibn al-Furat's third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizierate</a> in 923-924 he was sent into quasi-exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>, but a military coup brought him back and the vizier and his son were executed. Mu'nis now had a decisive voice in naming viziers, and his rift with al-Muqtadir widened until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> plotted to kill him in 927. That year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> surprised his army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> and killed 400 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 928, after his favourite Ali ibn Isa was dismissed, Mu'nis deposed al-Muqtadir and set up al-Qahir, but reversed this within days. In 933 al-Qahir lured him and his main lieutenants to the palace and had them executed. Michael Bonner wrote that he &quot;kept the remnants of the army together and saved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> on several occasions&quot;, and Harold Bowen judged his influence on the whole good but too weak to halt the decline. His seizure of power and the killing of a caliph, the first since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchy-at-samarra">Anarchy at Samarra</a>, set a precedent: military strongmen later contended for the title amir al-umara until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 946.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mu'nis al-Muzaffar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=902" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Mu'nis al-Muzaffar"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6016 4653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6016 4653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=902">What was here in 902 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
